TITLE:: PltMeter
summary:: Peak and RMS level meter, one bar per channel
categories:: Libraries>PlotLib
related:: Overviews/PlotLib, Classes/PltSpectrum, Classes/SendPeakRMS

DESCRIPTION::
A vertical bar per channel: RMS filled, peak as a line, the peak held for a moment
above it, and a red mark at the top when a channel has hit full scale.

Analysis runs on the server with link::Classes/SendPeakRMS:: at the tail of the root
node, so code::In.ar:: sees what everything else has written, and arrives as OSC.

code::pushFrame:: does the same job from the language, so the view can be driven
from any other source, and can be tested without a server.

note::
code::SendPeakRMS:: applies its own decay to the peak, and its default is 3 seconds,
which makes the peak line fall slowly and duplicates the hold line. PltMeter sets
strong::peakLag:: to 0.1 instead and lets strong::holdTime:: do the holding.
::

The analysis is rebuilt through link::Classes/ServerTree::, so it comes back after
cmd-period and after a reboot. Without that a meter goes on displaying the last
level it received, and a frozen meter looks exactly like a working one.

SECTION:: Compared with s.meter

link::Classes/ServerMeter:: covers a different job: it is the fixed monitor for a
whole server, inputs and outputs together. PltMeter is a meter you point at a bus
and configure.

table::
## strong::  || strong::s.meter:: || strong::PltMeter::
## busses || inputs and outputs in one window || one bus range per instance, any bus
## scale || fixed, -80 to 0, two labels || strong::floorDb::, with real dB ticks
## update rate || 10 Hz, a class variable || strong::replyRate::, 20 by default
## peak decay || peakLag 3 s, fixed || strong::peakLag:: 0.1, plus a held line for strong::holdTime::
## clipping || colour change in the LevelIndicator || red line and a mark above the frame, and strong::clipping:: as a Boolean
## values in the language || none, they live in the widgets || strong::peaks::, strong::rmss::, strong::holds::
## driving it yourself || no || strong::pushFrame::
## after cmd-period || recovers || recovers
## window || fixed size, escape closes || resizable, escape closes, strong::alwaysOnTop::, strong::position::
## embedding in your own view || yes, ServerMeterView || no, it owns its window
## server synths || one pair per server, shared by every view || one synth per meter
::

So for watching a whole server, code::s.meter:: is still the right thing. Use this
one when the meter needs a scale, a bus of its own, its numbers available to code,
or a theme that matches the other plots.

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
argument:: numChannels
argument:: bus
Bus index to read from. 0 is the first output channel.
argument:: server
Defaults to code::Server.default::. Analysis starts at once if it is running,
otherwise call strong::start:: later.
argument:: title
argument:: width
Left out, it comes from strong::widthFor::, so a stereo meter is a narrow window
and a sixteen channel one is a wide one. Pass a number to override.
argument:: height

METHOD:: widthFor
The window width for a channel count: the two dB label margins plus
strong::barWidth:: and strong::barGap:: per channel.
code::
PltMeter.widthFor(2);      // 120
PltMeter.widthFor(8);      // 300
::

METHOD:: barWidth
METHOD:: barGap
The bar and the space beside it, in pixels, used to size the window. The drawing
itself divides whatever width the window has, so resizing keeps the bars
proportional.

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: start
Create the analysis synth and the responder. A no-op if already running.

METHOD:: stop
Free them. strong::close:: does this too.

METHOD:: pushFrame
One frame of levels, as linear amplitudes.
argument:: argPeaks
argument:: argRms

METHOD:: clipping
True while any channel has hit full scale within the hold time.

METHOD:: peaks
METHOD:: rmss
METHOD:: holds
Current values, as linear amplitudes.

METHOD:: floorDb
Bottom of the scale.
METHOD:: holdTime
Seconds a peak is held.
METHOD:: replyRate
Frames per second from the server.
METHOD:: peakLag
See the note above.
METHOD:: rmsColor
METHOD:: peakColor
METHOD:: clipColor
METHOD:: numChannels
METHOD:: bus
METHOD:: server

EXAMPLES::
code::
s.boot;
m = PltMeter(2, 0).front;             // the first two output channels
x = { PinkNoise.ar(0.3) ! 2 }.play;
x.free;
m.close;

// a wider meter, more headroom shown
m = PltMeter(8, 0, s, "eight channels", 560, 340).floorDb_(-72).front;

// driven from the language
m = PltMeter(4, 0, nil, "from anywhere").front;
m.pushFrame([1.0, 0.5, 0.12, 0.01], [0.7, 0.35, 0.08, 0.004]);
m.clipping;                           // true
::
